 **//* wireless_send_event(..) *///#define LED_FILE "/scripts/led.txt"/*
 * In core.c, linux/arch/arm/mach-ums
 *//* Static buffer for holding kernel crash across reboot *//* Read-only diagnostics remain valid after the one-shot bring-up. *//* used for send formatted string custom event IWEVCUSTOM *//* Format the custom wireless event *//* ARC's no-cache, TLB-bypass region where we have all our registers *//* Post command_set processing - propagate the changes in qcb into other structures *//* This will happen for PCIe host *//*
 * check that MuC has completed its own initialisation code, and the boot complete
 * hostlink message has been processed.
 *
 * 1 on success, 0 otherwise
 *//*
 * Check that vap has been created successfully, 1 on success
 *//**
 * block until a condition is met, which is provided by check_func.
 * check_func returns 1 on successful condition.
 *
 * if booting the muc for the first time,
 * wlan (mac->data) will not be available yet.
 * assume qcb->macs[0] is the mac of interest here.
 *
 * returns 0 on successful condition, -1 on timeout, crash, or
 * mac/wlan/shared_params not properly initialized
 *//* once alive, qw should be initialised *//* There is no bond EMAC for PCIe EP board *//* Act on the opmode if set *//* Second argument must be all digits *//* deleted DFS/Radar related timers *//* address range [begin, end], inclusive *//*
 * List below is based on "golden values" registers file
 * Commented memory ranges contains "holes" which are not
 * allowed to be read or they're intentionally missed by
 * qregcheck tool.
 *//*{0xE1007FFC, 0xE1038FFF, "switch regs"},*//*{0xE2000000, 0xE200000F, "SPI0 regs"},*//*{0xE3100000, 0xE310009F, "AHB regs"},*//*{0xE9000000, 0xE9000bFF, "PCIE regs"},*//*{0xEA000000, 0xEA0003FF, "DMAC Conrol regs"},*//*{0xF9000000, 0xF90000FC, "I2C regs"},*//*
 * Variables for the register save/comparison logic. Two buffers for caching the values
 * and an index into the hardware registers structure for the currently measured set
 * of registers.
 *//* Address-> register count, with each register 32-bits *//* Inverse counter because seq file iterator uses reverse direction *//*
	 * Index starts from 1 to allow qdrv_dump_hwreg_one_reg_output compatibility with seq files output.
	 *//* Inverse counter because seq file iterator uses reverse direction *//* Inverse counter because seq file iterator uses reverse direction *//*
			 * If the buffer is allocated, do one final dump in the final index.
			 *//* For read and write pointers, the MSB is ignored */// if ddr addr, add the ddr bit otherwise assume addr// is correct (i.e. user has to correct for sram addr// convert from muc addr/* Check that we have all the arguments *//*
 * GPIO programs - separate from the LED programs
 *//*
	   * For WPS, just report thru printk.
	   * No reporting thru /proc/qdrvdata (qdrv_control_set_show, etc.)
	   *//*
		 * For WPS, we have "gpio set wps 4" and "gpio set wps 4 intr".
		 * Latter selects interrupt-based monitoring.
		 *//* RF register value is now returned from the MuC *//*
 * Calcmd format:
 *
 * Each calcmd is a sequence of U8's; thus each element is in the range 0 - 255.
 *
 * The first element identifies the calcmd.  Look in macfw/cal/utils/common/calcmd.h for symbolic
 * enums.  Example values include SET_TEST_MODE and GET_TEST_STATS.  Symbolic values are not used in
 * the Q driver.
 *
 * Second element is required to be 0.
 *
 * Third element is the total length of the calcmd sequence.  If no additional argument are present,
 * this element will be 4.  For each argument, add 2 to this element.
 *
 * Fourth element is also required to be 0.
 *
 * Remaining elements are the arguments, organized as pairs.  First element in each pair is the
 * argument index, numbered starting from 1 - NOT 0.  Second element is the argument value.  Thus
 * ALL calcmd input arguments (output arguments are also possible) are required to be 8 bit values.
 * Larger values (e.g. U16 or U32) have to be passed in 8-bit pieces, with Linux taking the value
 * apart and the MuC reassembling then.
 *
 * Output arguments are returned in the same buffer used to send the calcmd to the MuC.  See
 * GET_TEST_STATS (calcmd = 15) for an example.
 *//* Please do not delete for future use *//* SET_TEST_MODE *//* GET_RFIC_REG *///rssi_flag is 0 for EVM measurement//need to check specs of RFIC4 to find out dynamic range temp sensor/* Rx IQ cal cmd: print failed status*///GET_RFIC_REG;/* Get the private device data *///Cmd format : 11 0 4 0 ///* Check that we have all the arguments *//* Check that address is valid *//* Save the address for a memory read *//*
 * Push selected sysmsgs for capture and debugging
 * - currently only MuC messages are sent
 *
 * Add a debug message to the output buffer.
 * If the output buffer is full it is forwarded to the Ethernet driver.
 * A timer function also calls this function periodically so that data is not left in the
 * buffer for too long.
 *//*
 * Push TXBF pkt for capture and debugging
 *//*
		 * totalsize if the amount of payload:
		 * [wp struct] [data] * num watchpoints
		 * check that we're not requesting too much data, e.g. oversizing the debug packet
		 *//*
 * Display or enable Tx debugs
 *
 * Syntax:
 *   txdbg
 *   - display all qdrv_tx_ctr[] values
 *
 *   txdbg [<ctr> <cnt>] ...
 *   - print the next <cnt> QDRV_TX_DBG(<ctr>, ...) debug messages
 *   - any number of <ctr>/<cnt> pairs may be specified
 *//* Copy out any crash log, let the parse function deal with validity of the buffer *//*
		 * Format of buffer:
		 *	2 bytes: Header (HEADER_CORE_DUMP)
		 *	2 bytes: Length of the compressed logs (n)
		 *	n bytes: Compressed logs
		 *//* Check if the header exists *//* Strip the header and length while copying *//* Fill only the five OEM firmware regions after every range has passed. *//* Maybe never come here *//* neutralize VLAN group creation *//* set vlan-group {ifname} {vlan_id} [0|1] *//* Check if a specific address is given *//* setting something in shared parameters *//* Use an offset into our control structure *//* Propagate any parameters into sub-structures of the qcb. *//* Calculate avarage value for histogram *//* ------------------------------------------------------------------------ *//* ------------------------------------------------------------------------ *//* ------------------------------------------------------------------------ *//* Stats *//* Beamforming *//* 11n rates *//* 11ac rates *//* Only for debug - should be "off" in production code *//* Get the private device data *//* Show the return code from the previous command *//* Skip leading white space up to first argument *//* Check for empty string *//* Empty string!! *//* Save argument *//* Increment the number of arguments we have found *//* Skip argument *//* Remember the end of argument *//* Skip leading white spaces up to next argument(s) *//* Terminate argument *//* Check for end of arguments *//* Check if there are arguments left *//* Too many arguments *//* Get the private device data *//* Make sure it fits into our command buffer as a '\0' *//* terminated string.                                  *//* Copy to a buffer to make a proper C string *//* Kill '\n' if there is one *//* Parse the arguments *//* Make sure we got at least a command *//* Try to match the commands to the input *//* Call the function if we found a match *//* Qualified F355 lifetime: pin after policy, before a possible mutation. *//* Nothing to output *//* Get the number of items to read *//* Nothing to output *//* Return the number of items to read *//* Nothing to output *//* The iterator is done *//* Not valid any more *//* Nothing to output *//*
 * MU related commands:
 *    syntax: mu {set | get | dbg | clr} [sub-cmd] [mac_addr] [options]
 *
 *    1. set station group id and user_position
 *       mu set grp {mac_addr} {grp_id} {position}
 *    2. clear station group id
 *       mu clr grp {mac_addr} {grp_id} {position}
 *//* parse the cmd chains *//* parse subcmd & the parameters *//* Get the private device data *///hzw: debug print out the SRAM layout/* Teardown must complete its proc lifetime drain despite signals. *//* A request may have waited while module exit closed admission. *//**
