 *//*
 * IEEE 802.11 input handling.
 *//* wireless_send_event(..) *//* SIOCGIWTHRSPY *//* ARPHRD_ETHER *//*
 * Decide if a received management frame should be
 * printed when debugging is enabled.  This filters some
 * of the less interesting frames that come frequently
 * (e.g. beacons).
 *//*
 * Emit a debug message about discarding a frame or information
 * element.  One format is for extracting the mac address from
 * the frame header; the other is for when a header is not
 * available or otherwise appropriate.
 *//* IEEE80211_DEBUG *//**
 * Given a node and the RSSI value of a just received frame from the node, this
 * function checks if to raise an iwspy event because we iwspy the node and RSSI
 * exceeds threshold (if active).
 *
 * @param vap: VAP
 * @param ni: sender node
 * @param rssi: RSSI value of received frame
 *//* PS-POLL frame in State 1 *//* Packet from unknown source - send deauth. *//*
			 * Corner case
			 * AP may have changed mode to STA but we are still unconscious.
			 * If Deauthentication frames from AP are dropped here, we have no chance
			 * to disconnect with AP.
			 *//* Check the last rate since the list was sorted *//*
 * Process a received frame.  The node associated with the sender
 * should be supplied.  If nothing was found in the node table then
 * the caller is assumed to supply a reference to ic_bss instead.
 * The RSSI and a timestamp are also supplied.  The RSSI data is used
 * during AP scanning to select a AP to associate with; it can have
 * any units so long as values have consistent units and higher values
 * mean ``better signal''.  The receive timestamp is currently not used
 * by the 802.11 layer.
 *
 * Context: softIRQ (tasklet)
 *//* Variable to track whether the node inactive timer should be reset *//* XXX adjust device in sk_buff? *//* undefined *//*
	 * In monitor mode, send everything directly to bpf.
	 * Also do not process frames w/o i_addr2 any further.
	 * XXX may want to include the CRC
	 *//*
	 * Bit of a cheat here, we use a pointer for a 3-address
	 * frame format but don't reference fields past outside
	 * ieee80211_frame_min w/o first validating the data is
	 * present.
	 *//* Do not try to find a node reference if the packet really did come from the BSS *//* Try to find sender in local node table. *//*
					 * Fake up a node for this newly discovered
					 * member of the IBSS.  This should probably
					 * done after an ACL check.
					 *//* NB: stat kept for alloc failure *//*
			 * Validate the bssid.
			 *//* It can be a beacon from other network. Required for certification. *//* QTN_BG_SCAN *//* not interested in *//* not interested in *//* XXX catch bad values *//* QTN_BG_SCAN *//* duplicate, discard *//* XXX *//*
					 * In IEEE802.11 network, multicast packet
					 * sent from me is broadcasted from AP.
					 * It should be silently discarded for
					 * SIMPLEX interface.
					 *
					 * NB: Linux has no IFF_ flag to indicate
					 *     if an interface is SIMPLEX or not;
					 *     so we always assume it to be true.
					 *//*
				 * if it is broadcast by me on behalf of
				 * a station behind me, drop it.
				 *//* Decr ref count *//* XXX no power-save support *//*
			 * FIXME - QOS Null check added because Quantenna image
			 * currently doesn't set the to/from DS bits.
			 *//* check if source STA is associated *//* NB: caller deals with reference *//*
						 * Some 3rd party wds ap sends wds pkts with receiver
						 * addr as bcast/mcast which will be received by our ap
						 * and lead to a lot of deauth. But they just ignore our
						 * deauth frame. To avoid too much deauth messages, We can
						 * safely ignore them.
						 *//*
			 * If we're a 4 address packet, make sure we have an entry in
			 * the node table for the packet source address (addr4).
			 * If not, add one.
			 *//* Last call increments ref count if !NULL *//*
					 * node with source address (addr4) moved
					 * to another WDS capable station.
					 *//*
			 * Check for power save state change.
			 *//* XXX here to keep compiler happy *//*
		 * Handle privacy requirements.  Note that we
		 * must not be preempted from here until after
		 * we (potentially) call ieee80211_crypto_demic;
		 * otherwise we may violate assumptions in the
		 * crypto cipher modules used to do delayed update
		 * of replay sequence numbers.
		 *//*
				 * Discard encrypted frames when privacy is off.
				 *//* NB: stats+msgs handled in crypto_decap *///FIXME: This MUST be re-enabled - it could present a security hole.//Needs more thought.////RK-2009-11-24: this was commented out to allow WPA2 AES fragments//to pass through the slow driver path.//goto out;/*
		 * Next up, any fragmentation.
		 *//* Fragment dropped or frame not complete yet *//*
		 * Next strip any MSDU crypto bits.
		 *//* TDLS data encapsulated management frame *//*
		 * Finally, strip the 802.11 header.
		 *//* no longer valid, catch any uses *//* don't count Null data frames as errors *//* if sub type is NULL DATA or QOS NULL DATA, don't send to linux protocol stack *//* Only accept action frames and peer beacons for WDS *//*
				 * Only shared key auth frames with a challenge
				 * should be encrypted, discard all others.
				 *//* XXX *//*
				 * Discard encrypted frames when privacy is off.
				 *//* NB: stats+msgs handled in crypto_decap *//*if a sta receive a PS-POLL, a deauth should be sent*//* should not come here *//*
 * Determines whether a frame should be accepted, based on information
 * about the frame's origin and encryption, and policy for this vap.
 *//* encrypted eapol is always OK *//* cleartext eapol is OK if we don't have pairwise keys yet *//* cleartext eapol is OK if configured to allow it *//* cleartext eapol is OK if other unencrypted is OK *//* not OK *//*
		* Deny any non-PAE frames received prior to
		* authorization.  For open/shared-key
		* authentication the port is mark authorized
		* after authentication completes.  For 802.1x
		* the port is not marked authorized by the
		* authenticator until the handshake has completed.
		*//*
 * Context: softIRQ (tasklet)
 *//* Discard input from non-peer *//* No more vaps, reclaim skb *//*
 * This function reassemble fragments using the skb of the 1st fragment,
 * if large enough. If not, a new skb is allocated to hold incoming
 * fragments.
 *
 * Fragments are copied at the end of the previous fragment.  A different
 * strategy could have been used, where a non-linear skb is allocated and
 * fragments attached to that skb.
 *//* Quick way out, if there's nothing to defragment *//*
	 * Remove frag to ensure it doesn't get reaped by timer.
	 *//*
		 * Should never happen.  If the node is orphaned (not in
		 * the table) then input packets should not reach here.
		 * Otherwise, a concurrent request that yanks the table
		 * should be blocked by other interlocking and/or by first
		 * shutting the driver down.  Regardless, be defensive
		 * here and just bail
		 *//* XXX need msg+stat *//*
	 * Use this lock to make sure ni->ni_rxfrag is
	 * not freed by the timer process while we use it.
	 * XXX bogus
	 *//*
	 * Update the time stamp.  As a side effect, it
	 * also makes sure that the timer will not change
	 * ni->ni_rxfrag for at least 1 second, or in
	 * other words, for the remaining of this function.
	 *//*
	 * Validate that fragment is in order and
	 * related to the previous ones.
	 *//*
			 * Unrelated fragment or no space for it,
			 * clear current fragments
			 *//* If this is the first fragment *//* If more frags are coming *//*
				 * We need a continous buffer to
				 * assemble fragments
				 *//*
			 * Check that we have enough space to hold
			 * incoming fragments
			 * 1. Don't assume MTU is the RX frame size limit.
			 * 2. Don't assume original packet starts from skb->head, in case
			 * kernel reserve some bytes at headroom.
			 *//*
			 * We know we have enough space to copy,
			 * we've verified that before
			 *//* Copy current fragment at end of previous one *//* Update tail and length *//* Keep a copy of last sequence and fragno *//* we're done with the fragment *//* More to come *//* Last fragment received, we're done! *//*
	 * perform as a bridge within the vap
	 * - intra-vap bridging only
	 *//*
			 * Check if destination is associated with the
			 * same vap and authorized to receive traffic.
			 * Beware of traffic destined for the vap itself;
			 * sending it will not work; just let it be
			 * delivered normally.
			 *//* XXX statistic? *//* XXX insert vlan tag before queue it? *//* Max size address frames *//* Only copy hdrlen over *//*
		 * for TDLS Function, TDLS link with third-party station
		 * which is 3-address mode.
		 *//*
		 * for TDLS Function, associate with third-party AP
		 * which is 3-address mode.
		 *//* XXX does this always work? *//*
		 * Tack on 11g extended supported rate element.
		 *//*
 * Install received rate set information in the node's state block.
 *//*
		 * Tack on 11g extended supported rate element.
		 *//* XXX maybe a unique error? *//*
			 * To send the frame to the requesting STA we have to create a node
			 * for the station that we're going to reject.
			 *//* should not come here *//* always accept open authentication requests *//*
 * Send a management frame error response to the specified
 * station.  If ni is associated with the station then use
 * it; otherwise allocate a temporary node suitable for
 * transmitting the frame and then free the reference so
 * it will go away as soon as the frame has been transmitted.
 *//* XXX statistic *//* XXX TODO: add statistics *//*
	 * NB: this can happen as we allow pre-shared key
	 * authentication to be enabled w/o wep being turned
	 * on so that configuration of these can be done
	 * in any order.  It may be better to enforce the
	 * ordering in which case this check would just be
	 * for sanity/consistency.
	 *//* NB: silence compiler *//*
	 * Pre-shared key authentication is evil; accept
	 * it only if explicitly configured (it is supported
	 * mainly for compatibility with clients like OS X).
	 *//* XXX maybe a unique error? *//* XXX *//* NB: don't return error so they rexmit *//* NB: don't send a response *//* XXX IEEE80211_SCAN_FAIL_STATUS *//* XXX could optimize by passing recvd challenge *//*
	 * Send an error response; but only when operating as an AP.
	 *//* XXX hack to workaround calling convention *//*
		 * Kick the state machine.  This short-circuits
		 * using the mgt frame timeout to trigger the
		 * state transition.
		 *//* Verify the existence and length of __elem or get out. *//* Reject empty ssid in association requests *//* unaligned little endian access *//*
 * Convert a WPA cipher selector OUI to an internal
 * cipher algorithm.  Where appropriate we also
 * record any key length.
 *//* NB: so 1<< is discarded *//*
 * Convert a WPA key management/authentication algorithm
 * to an internal code.
 *//* NB: so is discarded *//*
 * Parse a WPA information element to collect parameters
 * and validate the parameters against what has been
 * configured for the system.
 *//*
	 * Check the length once for fixed parts: OUI, type,
	 * version, mcast cipher, and 2 selector counts.
	 * Other, variable-length data, must be checked separately.
	 *//* NB: len is payload only *//* NB: iswapoui already validated the OUI and type *//* multicast/group cipher *//* unicast ciphers *//* key management algorithms *//* optional capabilities *//*
 * Convert an RSN cipher selector OUI to an internal
 * cipher algorithm.  Where appropriate we also
 * record any key length.
 *//* NB: so 1<< is discarded *//*
 * Convert an RSN key management/authentication algorithm
 * to an internal code.
 *//* NB: so is discarded *//*
 * Parse a WPA/RSN information element to collect parameters
 * and populate the rsn parameters in struct
 *//*
	 * Check the length once for fixed parts:
	 * version, mcast cipher, and 2 selector counts.
	 * Other, variable-length data, must be checked separately.
	 *//* multicast/group cipher *//* unicast ciphers *//* key management algorithms *//* optional RSN capabilities *//* XXXPMKID *//*
 * Parse a WPA/RSN information element to collect parameters
 * and validate the parameters against what has been
 * configured for the system.
 *//*
	 * Check the length once for fixed parts:
	 * version, mcast cipher, and 2 selector counts.
	 * Other, variable-length data, must be checked separately.
	 *//* multicast/group cipher *//* unicast ciphers *//* key management algorithms *//* optional RSN capabilities *//* XXXPMKID *//* unicast ciphers *//* key management algorithms *//* optional RSN capabilities *//*
 * This function is only used on STA and the purpose is to save the cipher
 * info filled in ASSOC_REQ and apply it to node allocation once association
 * response received.
 * If TKIP, need two entries; If others, only need one entry
 *//*
			 * fixed part for RSN IE. version, mcast cipher, and 2 selector counts.
			 * Other, variable-length data, must be checked separately.
			 *//*
			 * Check the length once for fixed parts: OUI, type,
			 * version, mcast cipher, and 2 selector counts.
			 * Other, variable-length data, must be checked separately.
			 *//* XXX do proper check for wraparound *//* NB: ACI not used *//* ETH_P_80211_RAW *//* NB: try first to preserve turbo *//* attempt a cancel *//* Compare with stored ie, return 0 if unchanged *//* Take the combination of IC and STA parameters *//* set HT capabilities *//* set channel width *//* Set power save mode - STA determines this, not the AP. *//* WDS power save unsupported *//* Default invalid PS mode to NONE *//* set SHORT GI options *//* Set STBC options *//* delayed block ack *//* Maximum A-MSDU size *//* DSSS/CCK mode in 40 MHz *//* PSMP support (only if AP supports) *//* set 40 MHz intolerant *//* set L-SIG TXOP support *//* set maximum A-MPDU size *//* set maximum MPDU spacing *//* set MCS rate indexes *//* set maximum data rate *//* set MCS parameters *//* set number of Tx spatial streams *//* Spatial stream from 1-8 = 3 (not supported) *//* Check if perticular stream is not supported by peer *//* Following BF related fields require cross merging hence clear them first *//* Take the combination of IC and STA parameters *//* Set VHT capabilities in the node structure *//* Compare with stored ie, return 0 if unchanged *//* Compare with stored ie, return 0 if unchanged *//* set primary channel *//* set byte 1 values *//* set the channel width and secondary channel offset *//* force 20MHz bw if secondary channel offset is unknown *//* XXX set the S-PSMP support *//* service level granularity *//* set byte 2 values *//* set byte 3 values *//* set byte 4 values *//* set byte 5 values *//* set basic rates *//* CBW = 20/40 MHz, Nss = 1, Nes = 1, EQM/ No EQM *//* CBW = 20/40 MHz, Nss = 2, Nes = 1, EQM *//* Enable RTS-CTS if HT-protection bit is set *//* RTS-CTS can be enabled only when Rev B and later is used *//* Disable RTS-CTS if HT-protection bit is not set	*
	 * and if RTS-CTS is in use currently		*//* RTS-CTS can be enabled only when Rev B and later is used *//* Compare with stored ie, return 0 if unchanged *//*
static int
ieee80211_setup_vhtcap(struct ieee80211_node *ni, u_int8_t *ie)
{
	return 1;
}
*//* save the original HT CAP IE *//* Take the combination of IC and STA parameters *//* set HT capabilities *//* set channel width *//* set power save mode *//* NOTE: only ever called when we're running as an AP - take the SM power save as
	 * being what the client advertises, NOT what the AP wants.
	 *//* Default to Dyanmic powersave if invalid value passed in the (re)association request *//* set SHORT GI options *//* Set STBC options *//* If STA is capable of receive more streams then we support for tx,
			   then limit our transmission to what we support *//* delayed block ack *//* Maximum A-MSDU size *//* DSSS/CCK mode in 40 MHz *//* PSMP support (only if AP supports) *//* set 40 MHz intolerant *//* set L-SIG TXOP support *//* set maximum A-MPDU size *//* set maximum MPDU spacing *//* set MCS rate indexes *//* set maximum data rate *//* set MCS parameters *//* set number of Tx spatial streams *//*
		 * Channel Width
		 *//*
		 * Secondary Channel Offset
		 *//* need to verify the essid in the frame is the same to our *//* need to verify the essid in the frame is the same to our *//* update extender bssid ie *//* need to verify the essid in the frame is the same to our *//* update extender bssid ie *//* Remove the CSA IE from beacons *//* CSA was received recently *//* XXX abuse? *//* Can be abused, but with no (to little) impact. *//* CS mode change has no influence on our actions since
			 * we don't respect cs modes at all (yet). Complain and
			 * forget. *//* keep ic_csa_count unzero to avoid 2nd csa trigger *//* CSA wasn't received recently, so this is the first one in
		 * the sequence. *//* keep ic_csa_count unzero to avoid 2nd csa trigger *//* XXX. Not the right place for such a definition *//* dst: Broadcast address *//* src: associated STA *//*
 * Process beacon/probe response frames in:
 *    o AP mode, to check for non-HT APs on same channel
 *    o station mode when associated, to collect state updates such as 802.11g slot time
 *    o monitor/sniffer node, to handle CSA events
 *    o WDS mode, to set peer node capabilities and rates
 *    o adhoc mode, to discover neighbors
 *    o any mode, when scanning
 *//* 802.11ac *//* negotiated rate set *//* negotiated ht rate set *//* update key peer WDS *//* 4 bytes compose an extcap value, lower byte is on lower 8-bit of extcap value *//* teardown the link and clear timer *//*
 * Parse the wireless header for a TDLS frame
 *//*
 * Parse the TLVs in a TDLS action frame or public tdls action frame
 * Returns 0 if successful, else 1.
 *//* Parse TLVs *//* Unsupported vendor IEs are silently ignored*//* TDLS debugging *//*
 * Process a public TDLS action frame
 *//* for validation scripts *//* OEM449 uses BSS geometry even while a scan has another current channel. *//* Finding the frquency range *//*
		       * The scanned 20M bandwidth AP shares same channel
		       * with the primary channel of current AP
		       *//*
		       * The scanned 40M bandwidth AP shares same primary channel
		       * and secondary channel with current AP
		       *//* Finding the frquency range *//* A present channel report owns its declared bytes before node mutation. *//* For element id and length *//*
 * Process a public action frame
 *//*
 * Process a TDLS Action Frame.
 * Note: these are management frames encapsulated in data frames
 *//* Parse fixed length fields *//* Process the frame *//* Flush any state from a previous association *//*
	 * If the station requested bridge mode but it is not advertised,
	 * restart.  This could happen if the client is using a stale
	 * beacon.
	 *//* Implicit BA flags for the STA *//* See whether to do 4 address LNCB encapsulation *//* LNCB with 4 addresses can only be done when the AP is a bridge. *//* No 4 addr packets if not bridge mode *//* Flush any state from a previous association *//* Implicit BA flags for the STA *//*
 * Handle an HT Action frame.
 *//* Check for channel width change *//* update node's recommended tx channel width *//*
		 * Parsing of the input SM PS action frame. This moves the station in and out
		 * of SM Power Save and also changes the mode when enabled (dynamic, static).
		 *//* action type, action category *//* Bit 0 - enabled/disabled *//* Bit 1 - static/dynamic *//* Disabled - don't care what the bit 1 value says *//* Change the mode with the MUC if different from current mode *//* Inform the MUC *//* Call the driver to do some stuff if it wants to *//*
 * True if the station is probably an Intel 5100 or 5300 device.
 *//*
 * Intel client type identification:
 * 620x:
 *   2x2, MCS 32, no TX STBC support, but support RX STBC: Action: send HT20 Channel Width Notification
 * 5100/5300:
 *   2x2(5100) or 3x3 (5300), MCS 32, no TX STBC support and no RX STBC support: Action: send HT20 Channel Width Notification,
 *   restrict to use 2 TX Chains, and use LGI for TX.
 * 6300:
 *   support 3x3, MCS 32, no TX STBC and SUPPORT RX STBC: treat it as normal client, Action: none
 *//* WAR: 11n LDPC in IOT mode is allowed only if:
	        iwpriv wifi0 set_ldpc_non_qtn 1
		Node is BRCM
		BRCM node indicated support for LDPC  *//* Keep LDPC disabled for non-QTN, non-BRCM devices for now *//*
	 * For Realtek devices, disable A-MSDU since we got better performance without A-MSDU,
	 * but this condition may be changed in future
	 *//*
		 * Old Intel devices do not Ack BBF QoS null frames. This can
		 * cause unstability and disconnections.
		 *//* Try to identify problem peers - may cause other peer stations to be blacklisted *//* Some Intel firmware versions support MCS 32 *//* Currently disabling BBF to intel. With BBF, it periodically sends delba every 30 seconds *//* TX BA does not work with Intel 5100 when running on HT40 mode *//* tell Muc only to use two 2 TX chain for Intel 5x00 client
			 * and turn off SGI for TX to them
			 *//* disable 5100 aggregation *//* Already a power of 2 *//*
 * Identify client during BA setup
 * For some IOT client, such as Intel6205 with some version, we cannot identify them during
 * association. But we can identify from BA setup. So it is necessary to update the node
 * information into MuC/AuC then.
 *//* let it decide whether to use any tweaks such as disable BBF *//* update into MuC/AuC if vendor changed *//*
 * Handle a Block Acknowledgement Action frame.
 *//* action type = 1 octet and category = 1 octet *//* ba parameter field is 1 octet ahead *//* For WDS, use larger buffer size for TID 0 to get more throughput *//* dialog = 1 octet and ba parameters = 2 octets *//* timeout = 2 octets *//* Call the driver to inform the MuC *//* send the response *//* action type = 1 octet and category = 1 octet *//* parameter field is 3 octet ahead *//* dialog = 1 octet *//* status = 2 octets *//*
			 * IOT WAR: 1SS/2SS BCM devices that advertise window size as 32 have trouble
			 * handling SW retries in between next AMPDU. Based on experiments, the results
			 * are smooth if window size is set to 16. Scoreboard size in AuC should also be
			 * 16 for these devices. The fix is mainly targeted to BCM 2x2 11n based ipads.
			 *//* Disable AMSDU for these phones *//* ba parameter = 2 octets *//* Call the driver to do some stuff if it wants to *//* action type = 1 octet and category = 1 octet *//* check current state *//* send autonomous response if enable=1 report=1, which is not against the standard *//* Quantenna CCA Extension *//* scale before sending *//*TODO: Do we need to send others time?  *//* check current state *//* send autonomous response if enable=1 report=1, which is not against the standard *//* check current state *//* send autonomous response if enable=1 report=1, which is not against the standard *//* sta statistics request field *//* sub element *//* check current state *//* send autonomous response if enable=1 report=1, which is not against the standard *//* check current state *//* send autonomous response if enable=1 report=1, which is not against the standard *//*
		 * as described in 10.11.9.1:
		 * if the STA has no beacon information avaiilable then the STA may
		 * either refuse the request or send an empty Beacon Report.
		 * here we choose to refuse this request if there's no candidates
		 * *//* if request wildcard bssid, at least one BSS could be reported *//* currently only frame count report is required *//* this counter is used incorrectly throughout the module, but not currently in use *//* autonomous report frame should be handled seperately *//* Quantenna CCA Extension *//* normal measurement report *//* handle with group ID used for Cisco *//* optional sub element *//* record the remote statistics to node *//* Skip the whole IE in case a single bad sub-IE encountered *//* for control IE below *//* Just skip unknown subelement types - for compatibility reasons *//* 11h measurement report *//* repeat one time *//* OFDM *//*
 * Check whether non-ERP protection required or not
 * @scan OBSS beacon data
 * @return TRUE if non-ERP protection is required, FALSE if not required
 *//* If non erp sta present in obss *//* If beacon is from b-only ap where supported rates are
	 * 1, 2, 5.5 and 11 Mbps
	 *//* Check all the rates in the supported rate IE. If
		 * any of the rate is not the B rate, then return 0,
		 * otherwise return 1
		 *//*
 * Sets the station's VHT capability based on received assoc resp frame
 * If peer AP is non-VHT TX AMSDU will be disabled in station
 *//* 802.11ac *//* WAR: Livebox AP is stricter in handling TX AMSDU packets *//* Get the total reservation count *//*
 * This function is used to verify the HESSID and Access Network Type.
 * Probe Response is sent only if these parameters are matched or is Wildcard
**//* Access Network Type *//*
	 * Interworking Element
	 * El.ID | Length | AccessNetworkOpt | VenueInfo     | HESSID
	 * 1Byte | 1Byte  | 1 Byte	     | 2B (Optional) | 6B (Optional)
	 *//*
 * This function is used to check VHT and HT capabilites presence.
 * returns 1 if VHT or HT capablities are present.
 * returns 0 if VHT or HT capablities are not present.
 **//*
 * Context: SoftIRQ
 *//* forward management frame to application *//*
		 * When STA disconnects and boots up as AP, the DEAUTH/DISASSOC frame sent may get
		 * lost and AP can't create a WDS link with it since it's still "associated".
		 * This may be recovered by force leaving "STA" once we detect it became AP.
		 *//*
		 * beacon/probe response frame format
		 *	[8] time stamp
		 *	[2] beacon interval
		 *	[2] capability information
		 *	[tlv] ssid
		 *	[tlv] supported rates
		 *	[tlv] country information
		 *	[tlv] parameter set (FH/DS)
		 *	[tlv] erp information
		 *	[tlv] extended supported rates
		 *	[tlv] power constraint
		 *	[tlv] WME
		 *	[tlv] WPA or RSN
		 *	[tlv] Atheros Advanced Capabilities
		 *	[tlv] Quantenna flags
		 *//* Agere element in beacon *//*
				 * XXX hack this since depending on phytype
				 * is problematic for multi-mode devices.
				 *//* XXX ATIM? *//* override standard WME IE */// As DS_PARAM IE is optional for 5 GHZ, double check here/* Explicitly ignore some unhandled elements *//* Pure legacy 5GHz APs should not have a channel check. *//* Exception to this is 'BG' APs. *//* FIXME: Won't work for pure legacy 11a beacons on the previous channel *//* The frame may have been received on the previous channel if the
			 * RX channel has been changed recently.
			 *//* QTN_BG_SCAN *//*
				 * Frame was received on a channel different from the
				 * one indicated in the DS params element id;
				 * silently discard it.
				 *
				 * NB: this can happen due to signal leakage.
				 *     But we should take it for FH phy because
				 *     the rssi value should be correct even for
				 *     different hop pattern in FH.
				 *//* IEEE802.11 does not specify the allowed range for
		 * beacon interval. We discard any beacons with a
		 * beacon interval outside of an arbitrary range in
		 * order to protect against attack.
		 *
		 * NB: Discarding beacon directly maybe not a good solution.
		 * It will lead to some IOT issues with AP whose beacon interval is not in this range,
		 * although most of AP will not set beacon interval out of this range.
		 *
		 *//*
		 * Count frame now that we know it's to be processed.
		 *//*
		 * When operating in station mode, check for state updates.
		 * Be careful to ignore beacons received while doing a
		 * background scan.  We consider only 11g/WMM stuff right now.
		 *//* record tsf of last beacon *//* silence compiler *//* XXX statistic *//* XXX statistic *//* update transmit power if necessary *//* do nothing *//* tell Muc to use ERP cts-to-self mechanism now *//* tell Muc to turn off ERP now *//* XXX statistic *//*
				 * NB: we assume short preamble doesn't
				 *     change dynamically
				 *//* XXX statistic *//* 11n *//* 802.11ac *//*
				 * Check the TIM. For now we drop out of
				 * power save mode for any reason.
				 *//* WDS/Repeater: re-schedule software beacon timer for STA *//* if previously BMPS detects swbmiss *//* it will disable power-saving temporary *//* to help beacon RX *//* now it is time to reenable power-saving *//*
			 * If scanning, pass the info to the scan module.
			 * Otherwise, check if it's the right time to do
			 * a background scan.  Background scanning must
			 * be enabled and we must not be operating in the
			 * turbo phase of dynamic turbo mode.  Then,
			 * it's been a while since the last background
			 * scan and if no data frames have come through
			 * recently, kick off a scan.  Note that this
			 * is the mechanism by which a background scan
			 * is started _and_ continued each time we
			 * return on-channel to receive a beacon from
			 * our ap.
			 *//* QTN_BG_SCAN *//*
		 * If scanning, pass information to the scan module.
		 *//* QTN_BG_SCAN *//* In two cases station scan list will be updated.
			 * 1. when 11ac_and 11n flag is set and only vht or ht capabilities
			 *	are present in Beacon/Probe response.
			 * 2. When 11ac_and_11n flag is not set.
			 *//* check beacon for non-ERP and non-HT non member protection mode
		 * non-ERP protection: OBSS non-ERP protection is set when
		 * a) non-ERP present bit is set in the OBSS AP
		 * b) B only AP is present
		 * non-HT non member protection is set when there is non HT BSS.
		 * Timer is used to reset the protection parameters after last
		 * non-ERP AP or non-HT BSS goes away.
		 *//*Legacy AP is present *//* First non-HT AP beacon received *//* Check non-ERP protection in 2 GHz band *//* First OBSS non-ERP AP beacon received *//* Set Use_Protection in ERP IE *//* To call ieee80211_add_erp function *//* tell Muc to use ERP cts-to-self mechanism now *//* Update beacon *//* Create a new entry in the neighbor table. *//*
				 * Copy data from beacon to neighbor table.
				 * Some of this information might change after
				 * ieee80211_add_neighbor(), so we just copy
				 * everything over to be safe.
				 *//* NB: must be after ni_chan is setup *//*
		 * prreq frame format
		 *	[tlv] ssid
		 *	[tlv] supported rates
		 *	[tlv] extended supported rates
		 *      [tlv] Atheros Advanced Capabilities
		 *//* WAR: Null-paddings are interpreted as null SSID IEs *//* XXX Atheros OUI support *//*
				 * XXX Cannot tell if the sender is operating
				 * in ibss mode.  But we need a new node to
				 * send the response so blindly add them to the
				 * neighbor table.
				 *//*
		 * auth frame format
		 *	[2] algorithm
		 *	[2] sequence
		 *	[2] status
		 *	[tlv*] challenge
		 *//* Consult the ACL policy module if set up *//* This will include broadcast deauth frame queued on BSS node *//* XXX not right *//*
		 * asreq frame format
		 *	[2] capability information
		 *	[2] listen interval
		 *	[6*] current AP address (reassoc only)
		 *	[tlv] ssid
		 *	[tlv] supported rates
		 *	[tlv] extended supported rates
		 *	[tlv] power capability
		 *	[tlv] supported channels
		 *	[tlv] wpa or RSN
		 *	[tlv] WME
		 *	[tlv] Atheros Advanced Capabilities
		 *	[tlv] Quantenna flags
		 *//* ignore current AP info *//* XXX verify only one of RSN and WPA ie's? *//* don't override RSN element
				 * XXX: actually the driver should report both WPA versions,
				 * so wpa_supplicant can choose and also detect downgrade attacks
                                 *//* For now just get the first WSC IE until we can handle multiple of these *//* Validate power capability *//* power capability:ID|LEN|MIN TX CAP|MAX TX CAP *//* check Power Cap IE Length *//* Validate association security credentials *//*
			 * Parse WPA information element.  Note that
			 * we initialize the param block from the node
			 * state so that information in the IE overrides
			 * our defaults.  The resulting parameters are
			 * installed below after the association is assured.
			 *//* XXX distinguish WPA/RSN? *//* Reject association if using TKIP and HT *//* discard challenge after association *//* 802.11 spec says to ignore station's privacy bit *//*
		 * If constrained to 11g-only stations reject an
		 * 11b-only station.  We cheat a bit here by looking
		 * at the max negotiated xmit rate and assuming anyone
		 * with a best rate <24Mb/s is an 11b station.
		 *//*
			 * Assoc request ignored when 11ac_and_11n flag is set and
			 * vht or ht capabilities are not-present in Assoc req/reassoc req packet.
			 *//* record capabilities, mark node as capable of HT *//*
				 * Flush any state from a previous association.
				 *//*
			 * Record WPA/RSN parameters for station, mark
			 * node as using WPA and record information element
			 * for applications that require it.
			 *//*
			 * Flush any state from a previous association.
			 *//*
			 * Record WPA/RSN parameters for station, mark
			 * node as using WPA and record information element
			 * for applications that require it.
			 *//*
			 * Flush any state from a previous association.
			 *//*
			 * Record OSEN parameters for station, mark
			 * node as using OSEN and record information element
			 * for applications that require it.
			 *//*
			 * Flush any state from a previous association.
			 *//*
			 * Record WME parameters for station, mark node
			 * as capable of QoS and record information
			 * element for applications that require it.
			 *//*
			 * Flush any state from a previous association.
			 *//*
			 * Flush any state from a previous association.
			 *//* Send TGf L2UF frame on behalf of newly associated station *//* Clean up old block ack state *//* Add the local implicit BA values *//* Quantenna peers are 4 address capable *//* Check if it is a broadcom station *//* if it an Intel 5x00/620x client, we need to mark the flag to use 2 tx chain only
		 * before informing MuC
		 *//* No BBF for legacy clients *//* TODO: Probably we need to filter out vendor sugn from ni_qtn_flags and move it to ni_vendor
		 * to remove duplicate signs of vendor.
		 *//*
		 * asresp frame format
		 *	[2] capability information
		 *	[2] status
		 *	[2] association ID
		 *	[tlv] supported rates
		 *	[tlv] extended supported rates
		 *	[tlv] WME
		 *//* XXX *//*
			 * Do not discard frames containing proprietary Agere
			 * elements 128 and 129, as the reported element length
			 * is often wrong. Skip rest of the frame, since we can
			 * not rely on the given element length making it impossible
			 * to know where the next element starts.
			 *//* override standard WME IE *//* check to see if need to set up ERP flag *//*
		 * Configure state now that we are associated.
		 *
		 * XXX may need different/additional driver callbacks?
		 *//*
		 * Honor ERP protection.
		 *
		 * NB: ni_erp should zero for non-11g operation
		 *     but we check the channel characteristics
		 *     just in case.
		 *//* tell Muc to use ERP cts-to-self mechanism now *//* tell Muc to turn off ERP now *//* Sanity check - make sure bridge mode advertisement is as expected *//* Implicit BA flags for the AP *//* 11n *//* Both Q-Station and AP should support HT *//* keep only the rates supported by both parties *//*
			 * Flush any state from a previous association.
			 *//* Apply the implicit BA parameters to the local structure.
		 * Prevents the sending of addba request to the other side of the link.
		 *//*For STA mode, record the start time of association with AP*//*
		 * deauth frame format
		 *	[2] reason
		 *//*
			 * Don't keep retrying the auth if we receive a deauth.
			 * Let wpa_supplicant choose the next action.
			 *//* Message to indicate STA sent the deauth *//* if a disassoc request is received in the un-auth state
		 * a deauth should be sent by the sta *//*
		 * disassoc frame format
		 *	[2] reason
		 *//*
 * Process a received ps-poll frame.
 *//* Okay, take the first queued packet and put it out... *//* XXX node stat *//* just in case *//*
	 * If there are more packets, set the more packets bit
