 *//*
 * BSD/OS-compatible network interface media selection.
 *
 * Where it is safe to do so, this code strays slightly from the BSD/OS
 * design.  Software which uses the API (device drivers, basically)
 * shouldn't notice any difference.
 *
 * Many thanks to Matt Thomas for providing the information necessary
 * to implement this interface.
 *//*
 * Compile-time options:
 * IFMEDIA_DEBUG:
 *	turn on implementation-level debug printfs.
 * 	Useful for debugging newly-ported  drivers.
 *//*
 * Initialize if_media struct for a specific interface instance.
 *//* IF don't-care bits *//*
 * Add a media configuration to the list of supported media
 * for a specific interface instance.
 *//*
 * Add an array of media configurations to the list of
 * supported media for a specific interface instance.
 *//*
 * Set the default active media. 
 *
 * Called by device-specific code which is assumed to have already
 * selected the default media in hardware.  We do _not_ call the
 * media-change callback.
 *//*
 * Device-independent media ioctl support function.
 *//*
	 * Set the current media.
	 *//*
		 * If no change, we're done.
		 * XXX Automedia may invole software intervention.
		 *     Keep going in case the the connected media changed.
		 *     Similarly, if best match changed (kernel debugger?).
		 *//*
		 * We found a match, now make the driver switch to it.
		 * Make sure to preserve our old media type in case the
		 * driver can't switch.
		 *//*
	 * Get list of available media and current media on interface.
	 *//* user requested max *//* XXX gcc *//*
		 * If there are more interfaces on the list, count
		 * them.  This allows the caller to set ifmr->ifm_count
		 * to 0 on the first call to know how much space to
		 * allocate.
		 *//*
		 * Don't allow the user to ask for too many
		 * or a negative number.
		 *//*
			 * Get the media words from the interface's list.
			 *//* oops! *//*
		 * We do the copyout on E2BIG, because that's
		 * just our way of telling userland that there
		 * are more.  This is the behavior I've observed
		 * under BSD/OS 3.0
		 *//*
 * Find media entry matching a given ifm word.
 *
 *//* XXX: right place for declaration? *//* must be in the same order as IFM_TYPE_DESCRIPTIONS *//*
 * print a media word.
 *//* Find the top-level interface type. *//* Any mode. *//*
	 * Check for the shared subtype descriptions first, then the
	 * type-specific ones.
	 *//*
	 * Look for shared options.
	 *//*
	 * Look for subtype-specific options.
	 *//* IFMEDIA_DEBUG *//* Derived OEM493 ExtCapIE127 payloadbyte2 bit3 update. Guard shortTLVs
 * and use the length actually copied; no management action handler is added. *//* Exact OEM RRMcapabilityIE: id70,len5, bit1 whenNeighborReport selected. *//* Actual scanner-owned adapter for OEM channel-set selection. The existing
 * validated channel tables supply regulatory/DFS state; templates grant no TX.
 * Shared scan counters are sampled scalars, not a coherent cross-MuC snapshot.
 * No allocation or sleeping operation occurs in scan completion/timer context.
 *//* Existing VAP caller holds its lifetime serialization. If startup refuses,
     * clear only the exact generation we reserved, never a later transaction. *//* Lock the shared scan cache while copying only bounded channel metrics.
     * No SSID/MAC/client data is recorded in source evidence or this snapshot. *//* Exact first-match semantics from OEM FUN_ram_00012f9c, using typed
 * fields rather than OEM raw offsets. Invalid bounds refuse before dereference.
 * Template selection is OEM FUN_ram_00012f18 for bands 0 and 1; out-of-range
 * band values fail explicitly instead of treating any nonzero as band5.
 *//* Recovered OEM 0x131a8: queue7, BW40 band24 uses ABOVE3/BELOW4.
 * OEM full queue restarts at0; this helper refuses instead of overwriting.
 * Invalid bandwidth leaves both queue and current_bandwidth unchanged.
 *//* Require one populated prefix followed by unused zero slots. *//* Exact nine-word defaults selected by OEM 0x13314. Channel-policy permissions
 * stay with the caller; constant template availability does not allow TX. *//* OEM helpers 0x13514/0x13598. Use signed 64-bit intermediates and refuse an
 * unrepresentable edge; the recovered static templates remain within bounds. *//* Exact 88-byte ARC hardware-loop body0x1488e..0x148e6. Ghidra omits this
 * non-procedure loop; original text bytes and ARC objdump supply the evidence.
 * ARC mpy/add/sub use low32 bits; preserve that wrap using unsigned arithmetic.
 * The final subtract is unconditional, even when entry->dfs is zero.
 *//* Exact OEM threshold0x418937: preserve divide-first truncation above it. *//* Order by the ARC signed branch without implementation-defined casts. *//* Validate the entire plan before changing transaction fields. *//* Caller must serialize this state and keep its owning radio alive. *//* OEM scan0x1361c: requests are returned to the caller. No scan is started
     * until a validated kernel/regulatory/lifetime adapter consumes them. *//* Does not release memory or buffers used by actual scan callbacks. The
     * kernel adapter must quiesce those callbacks before ending ownership. *//* Recovered two-row proximity policy at OEM0x85f74: min RSSI, span, weight.
 * The weakest neighbor keeps the previous row, as in the recovered loop.
 * These constants are ranking policy, never regulatory transmit permission. *//* Validate all potentially failing conversions before any metric update. *//* Exact two half-open overlap bands, using signed wide math to
             * keep subtraction/addition defined at domain boundaries. *//* Derived from exact OEM constant tables and REA 0x12f18/0x12f9c.
 * Source reconstruction only: no scan, ranking or regulatory acceptance.
 * Templates preserve OEM oddities byte for byte. No table grants channel use.
 *//* Per-radio transaction storage is caller-owned and bounded by original98
 * widest-band table records. It must not be allocated on a kernel stack. *//* Exact OEM constant 80-byte records; no runtime data. *//*-
